## Phase 8 — UI

I'd keep it restrained.

Something like:

**LEFT**

Change an entity:

`Brew Target Temperature`

**CENTRE**

Impact map:

`Parameter → Module → Behaviour → Requirement → Test`

**RIGHT**

Impact summary:

> Requirements: 2  
> Tests: 4  
> Documentation: 3  
> Components: 2

Click anything and see:

> **Why is this affected?**

Then show the relationship path.

That would be far more useful than throwing a gigantic force-directed graph at the recruiter.

---

## Phase 9 — Explainability

This may actually become one of the strongest pieces of the project.

The analyzer shouldn't merely say:

> `DOC-027 affected`

It should say:

> **DOC-027 potentially requires review because:**
>
> BrewTargetTemperature  
> → configures → TemperatureController  
> → implements → BrewTemperatureRegulation  
> → describedBy → DOC-027

The user can inspect the reasoning.

That demonstrates that you understand **traceability**, not just graph visualization.

---

## Phase 10 — Portfolio case study

The final narrative could be very simple:

**Problem**

Engineering changes propagate through interconnected systems, but their downstream effects can be difficult to identify.

**Approach**

I modelled a fictional industrial coffee machine as connected requirements, behaviours, components, software, configuration parameters, tests and documentation.

**Solution**

A relationship-driven impact analyzer traces potential downstream effects of a proposed change.

**Technical communication angle**

The system identifies documentation potentially requiring review and explains the relationship path that caused it to be surfaced.

**Learning**

Ontology design, knowledge graphs, graph traversal, traceability, impact analysis and explainable knowledge systems.

---

### And importantly: the finish line

We stop when the demo can convincingly do this:

> **Change one parameter.**

↓

> **Show me what might be affected.**

↓

> **Show me why.**

↓

> **Tell me which documentation might need review.**

That's enough.

No digital twin. No physics simulation. No giant ontology. No autonomous vehicle. No enterprise requirements-management platform. No need to turn the coffee machine into a perfect engineering model.

**The portfolio project exists to demonstrate relationship thinking.**

And I would start **Phase 1–3 with you before writing any code**. That's actually where most of your ontology learning is going to happen.
